{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1407876-potential-house-rule-alternate-to-motes-and-willpower","id":1407876,"title":"[Potential House Rule] Alternate to Motes and Willpower","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-13T01:19:17-07:00","id":1407876,"title":"[Potential House Rule] Alternate to Motes and Willpower","content":"So, I'm generally speaking, I find most suggestions of &quot;X does Exalted better than Exalted&quot; to be fairly uncompelling. Usually other RPGs do what some people want out of Exalted better than Exalted, but at the cost of dropping something I like. That said, Exalted (any of the editions) is still a beast of a game.<br /> <br /> One of the things I find hardest with Exalted in play is the resource tracking of things like willpower and motes, and how no matter how hard the various editions have tried, they're frequent a matter of attrition and careful management in a way that's not necessarily fun for tactically minded players, and unfun for players that just want to do cool stuff.<br /> <br /> Something that caught my eye recently was someone talking about Kamigakari (a Japanese RPG from '13 that was just released in official translation in English after a... rocky... Kickstarter process) in the &quot;This could do Exalted!&quot; way. Personally, I don't think Kamigakari could do Exalted much justice because it's got a lot of D&amp;D in it in terms of a high mechanical focus on combat and then everything else is grouped under a simple roll. So no real support for non-combat that's essential for a lot of Exalted character types.<br /> <br /> That said, Kamigakari has something in common with Exalted: characters end up with a big list of &quot;powers&quot; that the game gives differing costs to to helps to balance which ones you're going to use when. However it doesn't rely on having a big pool of points (or what's more frustrating with Exalted multiple pools of points) for this.<br /> <br /> It uses something it calls the Spirit Pool. It's a pool of dice (a set number that can be increased as your character gets number) that you roll at the start of a scene, and in action heavy scenes you reroll &quot;spent&quot; dice at the start of every turn. Spirit Dice are used in two different ways: Influence and activating powers.<br /> <br /> Influence doesn't &quot;spend&quot; your Spirit dice, but you can usually only Influence once per roll. In basic function, Influence occupies a similar place towards WP on rolls, or basic Charms like Excellencies. Influence lets you swap a die from your roll with a die from your Spirit Pool. The obvious use is to flip a failure into a success; though that quickly depletes the value of your Spirit Pool. If you roll really well, you can always reduce your roll by swapping a bad die from the Spirit Pool for a good one from your roll to recharge it a bit. Getting into deeper tactics gets into how you spend Spirit dice.<br /> <br /> Spending Spirit dice on powers means fulfilling the cost rating on the power. Powers can cost single dice (either specific numbers, an even number, or an odd number), pairs (any or specific), &quot;steps&quot; (n, n+1, again any of a size or specific ones), or combinations of them. A basic utility power you use a lot is probably going to cost an Even or an Odd. Powers that can one-shot PC level characters might have highly specific costs like an Odd and a pair of 6s. There's a balance between spending on low cost powers (since in high action means rerolling spent dice frequently), and saving up for your better powers, with Influence letting you push things towards what you need.<br /> <br /> I'm not really looking for a direct 1:1 translation, since Kamigakari uses a sum of dice rolls + mods &gt; TN system and I don't want to get rid of Exalted's success system.<br /> <br /> But I'm trying to come up with a basic framework to do something similar so people aren't tracking multiple big pools of points, and instead power use is more dynamic. I find the general concept simple enough for casual play, and deep enough for players that want to get into the tactics and odds. It also seems like it's pretty easy to have some more straightforward &quot;tier&quot; work for people that want the Solar &gt; Celestial &gt; Terrestrial divide to be stronger or weaker (Solars getting +2 Spirit dice, and Celestials getting +1, for example isn't a huge number difference even if it's a pretty potent difference in application without needing to make Terrestrial stuff &quot;worse&quot; but you could dial it up if you like the gap wider).<br /> <br /> The biggest thing I'm struggling with is the value of Influence hinges summing dice values, where the count successes part (baring dice-tricks in Charms) doesn't really care if you rolled a 10 and all 2s, or two 9s and a smattering of 3s, 4s and 5s. If you just treat the Spirit Pool like a standard Exalted dice pool, it mostly works, but it seems like the dice manipulation aspect loses a lot<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-13T05:27:08-07:00","id":1407901,"title":"","content":"Hmm. Maybe you could say that for influence you can either swap a die <i>or</i> spend one to add it's value to another die in the pool.<br /> <br /> And I could see excellencies working by just spending that many dice from the spirit pool. Just get your regular pool, and then pick up as many dice as you like from spirit and roll those too. As resources go it doesn't get more intuitive than that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-13T14:18:33-07:00","id":1408023,"title":"","content":"One of the appeals of Influence is that you don't spend to use it, so you're free to give rolls adjustments all the time, but it's rarely a huge boost. To an extent, it somewhat fills the role that the 3rd Excellency from 2e did: remove the worry that you might flub a basic roll you didn't even think was worth spending motes on since you can do so after the fact.<br /> <br /> Though I had a thought:<br /> <br /> What would you think of something like swapping dice, and adding the difference to one of the other dice? So if you swap a 1 in for a 5, you can add 4 to one of others?<br /> <br /> It would keep the Influence as not-spending, and the deeper tactical side of how Influence and Costs play off each other, but it feels like Influence would be more useful for making sure you can add a success to the roll, and also get rid of things like 1s that Charms might use against you.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-13T14:52:03-07:00","id":1408035,"title":"","content":"Hmm. It sounds like a very cool mechanic, but I'm not sure there's a good way to patch it into Exalted--if I'm understanding it correctly, you'd have to write new costs for every Charm. (Unless you could come up with some universal rule, like &quot;in place of spending X motes, spend spirit dice whose values add up to X or higher,&quot; but I don't know how you'd really go about that)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12314-grod_the_giant","name":"Grod_the_giant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12314_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-13T15:02:38-07:00","id":1408036,"title":"","content":"Actually patching it in would be it's own beast of a thing to do yes. I'm trying to figure out if the mechanic would even work in the rest of the Exalted designed paradigm to make the effort of figuring out costs worth it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T09:02:16-07:00","id":1408174,"title":"","content":"Here's my first draft : <br /> <br /> At the beginning of every scene, characters rolls their Spirits Pools (which is a fixed amount I have yet to determine, let's assume for the moment it's between 5 and 10). They have a mote pool, which is close to mote pools of Exalted Essence (probably around 5 let's say). Spirit Pool is replenished only (except Charms maybe) by consuming a mote of essence. <br /> <br /> Now during play, after any roll, you can decide to pair a dice of your roll with a dice from the spirit pool. You can only make the following pairs : A failed dice with a failed dice, a success dice with a success dice, and a double success dice with a double success dice. A failed dice pair reward 2 successes, a success dice pair reward 1 success, and a double success dice pair reward a WillPower point. I assume that the WP system is slightly reworked to start with less WP and you regain them less easily. <br /> <br /> The particularity/problems of the system : <br /> this changes the way Charm order function since you can choose to change scores after the roll. <br /> The rolls are more averaged and less explosives <br /> I don't know yet how to manage mote regen<br /> I don't know yet how to differentiate tiers of Exalted (probably changing their Spirit Pool size, or having them reward less success depending on the pair they use, for example for terrestrial a failed dice pair could award only 1 success).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T11:10:01-07:00","id":1408187,"title":"","content":"Personally, I'm hoping to replace motes and willpower (or at least willpower as a spendable resource on Charms, though I'd probably want to more to Willpower as a more direct social health thing than keep WP points just for resisting social stuff). Keeping motes and adding the spirit pool like that feels like it's just going to add steps that detract from &quot;just do cool stuff&quot; players fun by having even more to do, and the deeper tactical players are just going to have more pieces to sort through to have their fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T11:35:43-07:00","id":1408196,"title":"","content":"Yeah okay I see your point. I was thinking lowering the mote pool excessively would satisfy you but we are coming at this problem with different perspectives I think.<br /> <br /> Here's a second attempt : <br /> <br /> At the beginning of every scene, characters rolls their Spirits Pools (which is a fixed amount I have yet to determine, let's assume for the moment it's between 5 and 10). The pool can only be resplenished once it is falls under a certain threshold. Once the pool is empty, it is refilled to it's original number, -1 for each time you refilled it this scene, and -1 for each WP point you accumulated this scene (see below).<br /> <br /> Whenever you make a roll, you can remove any dice from the Spirit Pool if its value is between your lowest and highest dice rolled for the action you are making, and add a success to your roll. Any dice that is not in that interval can be &quot;banked&quot;, and used later as a WP point. (So WP to activate Charms are only obtainable during the same scene you are using it). But this dice can no longer go into the Spirit Pool for the scene.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T12:49:51-07:00","id":1408222,"title":"","content":"I admit that for a while I've been of the opinion that Exalted doesn't need Motes AND Willpower, and I'd be all in favor of reworking the system to just use Motes to power effects. Exalted Essence, from the previews I've seen, will only use Motes. I've only glanced at Kamigakari, admittedly, but rolling and banking successes at the start of a scene for Exalted doesn't sound super great to me? I dunno, it'd probably be less overwhelming if they took Motes and took a look at Mote Costs and how many Motes we actually need.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}